WEBVTT
TITLE Dynamic Programming — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Here is fib(6) written the way the definition reads: fib of n is fib of n minus one plus fib of n minus two.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Evaluate it literally and the tree branches twice at every node. For n equal to six that is 25 calls.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
But there are only 7 different questions in that tree: fib of 0 up to fib of 6. Everything else is a repeat.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
So the waste is not the recursion. The waste is forgetting. Keep the answers and the same definition costs seven steps.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Memoisation: before computing a state, look in the table. If the answer is there, return it; otherwise compute once and store it.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Each of the seven states is computed exactly once, so the running time becomes the number of states times the work per state.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Tabulation is the same table filled the other way round: start at the base cases and fill forward until the answer appears.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Same recurrence, same table, two spellings. What makes it dynamic programming is the table, not the recursion.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Question one: what is a state? It is the exact set of numbers that, together, pin down one subproblem.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Question two: what choices does the state allow? Knapsack allows take or skip. LCS allows drop a character from either side.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Question three: write the transition, and question four: write the base case. Those two lines are the whole recurrence.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Question five: in what order may the table be filled? Every state must be filled after the states it reads.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Four items, capacity 7. The state is (items considered, capacity left), so the table has five rows and eight columns.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
For each cell there are two candidates: the row above, which skips the item, and the item value plus the cell one row up and one item-weight left.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Filling row by row, the bottom-right corner reads 9, reached by taking I2 and I3, weights 3 and 4.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Value per weight would have taken I4 and I1 for 8. The table finds 9 because it compares combinations, not ratios.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
LCS of AGGTAB and GXTXAYB. State: how many characters of each string are still in play.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
When the two current characters match, add one to the diagonal. When they differ, take the better of dropping one character from either side.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The corner holds 4. That is the length, and the length is all the table stores.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Walk back along the matches and the arrows, and the letters spell GTAB. Same table, one extra pass.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A fill order is legal only if every cell is written after the cells it reads. For knapsack and LCS that is any order that goes up and left.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Interval problems break that. A cell for the interval i to j reads intervals shorter than itself, which are neither above nor left.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The fix is to loop over length first, then over the starting index. Length two, then three, then four.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
So the general rule: draw the dependency arrows for one cell, and order the loops so the arrows always point backwards.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Four matrices with dimensions 40 by 20, 20 by 30, 30 by 10 and 10 by 30. The order of multiplication changes the cost, not the result.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
The state is the interval of matrices from i to j, and the choice is where to split that interval.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Filling by length, the top-right cell ends at 26000, and the page checks that against every possible parenthesisation.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
A second table records the split, so the parenthesisation can be printed. Cost table plus choice table is the standard DP pattern.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Longest increasing subsequence of 3, 2, 6, 4, 5, 1. State: the index where the subsequence starts.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Filling from the right gives 3, 3, 1, 2, 1, 1, so the answer is 3 and one witness is 2, 4, 5.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
This is a genuine DP: n states, n work per state, n squared in total. GATE 2011 asked which paradigm such an algorithm uses.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The same shape solves the longest decreasing, the longest bitonic and the longest alternating versions: change the comparison, keep the table.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Floyd-Warshall asks: what is the shortest path from i to j using only vertices from the first k as intermediates?

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Allowing vertex 1 first, then 2, then 3. Each step either keeps the old distance or routes through the new vertex.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
On this graph the distance from 0 to 3 is 8 directly, and allowing vertex 2 drops it to 5 through 1 and 2.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
The same trick gives the longest path in a DAG, if you relax in topological order instead of by vertex number.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Cost is states times work per state. Knapsack has n plus one times W plus one states and two choices each.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Since a row reads only the row above it, one row of the table is enough. Space drops from n times W to W.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Loop capacity upwards and the item is reused: the array reports 9, which takes the weight-2 item three times.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Descending capacity reads only cells from the previous item, so it matches the full table exactly.

41
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.500
Two questions decide the paradigm. Does an optimal solution contain optimal solutions to subproblems, and do those subproblems repeat?

42
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:15.000
Greedy needs a third property: some optimal solution agrees with the locally best choice. Without a proof of that, greedy is a guess.

43
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.500
Six problems, six states: capacity and item, two prefix lengths, an interval, an index, two vertices plus an intermediate, a sum.

44
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:30.000
Carry this out of the lesson: name the state, write the transition and the base case, then say which order fills the table.

